From: locateanything-perf
Subject: two attention-implementation hooks pin themselves to the transformers
         4.x signature, so the model will not construct under transformers 5.x

nvidia/LocateAnything-3B, modeling_locateanything.py + modeling_qwen2.py.
Apply inside the downloaded model directory:

    patch -p0 -d $LA_MODEL < patches/05-transformers5-attn-impl-signature.patch
    rm -rf "$HF_HOME/modules/transformers_modules"     # REQUIRED, see below

WHAT IS WRONG

    >>> AutoModel.from_pretrained(model, trust_remote_code=True)
    File ".../transformers/modeling_utils.py", line 1355, in __init__
        self.config._attn_implementation_internal = \
            self._check_and_adjust_attn_implementation(
    TypeError: LocateAnythingPreTrainedModel._check_and_adjust_attn_implementation()
               got an unexpected keyword argument 'allow_all_kernels'

The checkpoint overrides two PreTrainedModel hooks so that the string "magi"
survives validation -- the model ships its own MAGI attention kernel, which is
not one of the implementations transformers knows about, so an unmodified
transformers rejects it before the model is ever built.

Both overrides copy the base signature of the day and forward it positionally:

    def _check_and_adjust_attn_implementation(self, attn_implementation,
                                              is_init_check=False):
        ...
        return super()._check_and_adjust_attn_implementation(
            attn_implementation, is_init_check)

transformers 5.x grew a third parameter:

    4.57.1   _check_and_adjust_attn_implementation(self, attn_implementation,
                                                   is_init_check=False)
    5.12.1   _check_and_adjust_attn_implementation(self, attn_implementation,
                                                   is_init_check=False,
                                                   allow_all_kernels=False)

and calls it with keywords. The override does not accept the new keyword, so
construction fails with a TypeError before a single weight is read. It is not
that the model needs an old transformers -- it is that these two methods
hard-code an argument list they do not use.

Note the method immediately above each of them in the same file:

    def _autoset_attn_implementation(self, config, *args, **kwargs):
        ...
        return super()._autoset_attn_implementation(config, *args, **kwargs)

The checkpoint's own author already solved this for the sibling hook and did
not carry it across. This patch does exactly that and nothing more.

WHY *args/**kwargs RATHER THAN ADDING allow_all_kernels

Neither override reads is_init_check or allow_all_kernels; both exist only to
intercept the literal "magi" and pass everything else through untouched. A
pass-through wrapper should not restate the signature it is wrapping -- that is
what made it break, and adding the 5.x parameter by name would pin it to 5.x
the same way it is currently pinned to 4.x. Forwarding opaquely means the
override keeps working on 4.57.1, on 5.12.1, and on whatever the signature
becomes next.

WHY THE CACHE MUST BE CLEARED

trust_remote_code does not execute the files in the model directory. It copies
them to $HF_HOME/modules/transformers_modules/<name>/<hash>/ and imports from
there. Patch the model directory without clearing that copy and nothing you
changed runs -- from_pretrained keeps raising the same TypeError from a path
you did not edit.

VERIFIED

    transformers 5.12.1, torch 2.11.0+cu130, RTX 3090:
      before: TypeError at modeling_utils.py:1355, no model
      after:  LocateAnythingForConditionalGeneration constructs and runs

